S("D.  RQ4: Scale and Data")
P("**Larger zero-shot model.** gpt-oss-120b on 100 respondents (774 rows) scores 27.6% (24.9 to 30.3). That is 2.3 points below the majority class (30.0%, not significant), against 18.7% for the 7B model on the same rows, and 11.8 points below the fine-tuned 7B model (7.1 to 16.5). It is a different and larger model and we did not vary scale within a family, so we cannot attribute the difference from the 7B model to scale. It sits at the majority class, not beyond it.")
P("**Labelled data.** Classical models need a fair amount of labelled data to beat the majority class. Averaged over five random subsets, logistic regression is at the majority level for N up to 100 respondents (30.5% against 29.1% at N = 100), exceeds it by 3.9 points at N = 400 (33.7%) and by 6.4 at N = 1,600 (36.4%). GBM clears the majority class in accuracy only at about 1,600 respondents (34.6%) and still has a worse NLL than the majority class there (1.69 against 1.59). On the same 200 test respondents, the fine-tuned 7B model (trained on 23,999 respondents) scores 37.7%, slightly above logistic regression trained on 1,600 respondents (36.4%), a difference within the sampling error of 200 respondents. Whether LLM adaptation is more sample-efficient than classical models in this low-data regime is the open question most likely to favour LLMs. We could not test it reliably with 7B models on our hardware, and a small-model (0.5B) attempt trained on up to 100 respondents collapsed to a near-constant answer, so we draw no conclusion from it.")
S("E.  WVS-7 India Replication")
T("Table VI. WVS-7 India, 15 items: zero-shot LLM accuracy against baselines on the same rows (95% respondent-bootstrap CIs). Entries give baseline accuracy; LLM minus baseline.",
  [["Row sample", "Gemini", "gpt-oss-120b", "Qwen2.5-7B"],
   ["Respondents / rows", "30 / 421", "40 / 556", "120 / 1,716"],
   ["LLM accuracy (%)", "25.7 [20.8, 30.5]", "23.0 [19.6, 26.7]", "22.1 [20.0, 24.4]"],
   ["Majority class", "20.9; +4.8 [-0.7, +10.2]", "22.3; +0.7 [-3.7, +5.1]", "24.9; -2.7 [-5.6, +0.1]"],
   ["Cell mode (6 attributes)", "20.4; +5.2 [-0.5, +10.9]", "21.2; +1.8 [-2.9, +6.4]", "25.6; -3.4 [-6.5, -0.4]"],
   ["Logistic (14 attributes)", "25.9; -0.2 [-5.2, +5.1]", "27.9; -4.9 [-9.4, -0.2]", "28.8; -6.7 [-9.9, -3.4]"],
   ["GBM (14 attributes)", "35.2; -9.5 [-15.6, -4.4]", "34.4; -11.3 [-16.9, -6.2]", "37.5; -15.3 [-18.7, -11.9]"]], [4.2, 3.9, 3.9, 3.9])
P("Across the 144 screened items the majority class reaches 45.5% and the cell mode 45.4%, against 46.9% for logistic regression and 50.1% for GBM, so demographics add only 1.4 and 4.6 points. On the 15 evaluated items (Table VI), Gemini 3.1 Flash Lite (30 respondents) scores 25.7% and gpt-oss-120b (40) scores 23.0%, both within sampling error of the majority class and 9.5 and 11.3 points below GBM. Zero-shot Qwen2.5-7B (120 respondents, 1,716 rows) scores 22.1%, which is 2.7 points below the majority class (95% CI -5.6 to +0.1) and 15.3 below GBM. Its group differences show no directional validity (r = -0.03 over 345 cells; GBM 0.64), the profile changes accuracy by -1.3 points (-4.2 to +1.5), and on seven of the 15 items (nine at a 90% threshold) it gives the same answer to nearly every respondent. The 15 items come from an earlier run and their own majority class is 21 to 25%, well below the 144-item figure, so each LLM is compared with the majority class on the same rows. Subgroup gaps cannot be told apart from random group assignment in any of 12 API-model tests (11 of 12 with p > 0.05, none after Holm correction) or in the 120-respondent run (smallest p = 0.082). Detection thresholds range from 4.5 to 26.7 points, above most of the gaps seen on Pew (1 to 2.5 points).")

H("V. Discussion")
P("**What the profile does.** A zero-shot model does not ignore the profile. It varies with it far more than real answers do, but in directions that agree with the data only weakly, and the net effect on exact-match accuracy is slightly negative. That fits persona effects being small and noisy [@hu2024] and the over-determination reported in [@chen2026]. We did not vary prompts, so we cannot say whether richer or better-worded demographics would help.")
P("**What fine-tuning does.** Fine-tuning on respondent-level data gives item marginals and group differences that approach a classifier's without reaching them. Accuracy ends 2.1 points below GBM and 0.5 below logistic regression, directional validity on the full fold is 0.82 against 0.91, and the two models agree on 72% of predictions. That no gain is detectable on unseen items is compatible with an item-specific mapping from profile to answer. Because of the port, the number of items and the narrow training set, it is not evidence that no transfer is possible.")
P("**Where LLM respondents could add value.** On this evidence, not as substitutes for labelled data on the same question, since matched classifiers are cheaper and more accurate, and not, at 7B with narrow fine-tuning, as predictors for new questions. The settings left open are the ones our design cannot test: richer text-conditioned personas, much broader fine-tuning across many items, and aggregate distribution estimation from probabilities rather than argmax.")
P("**Evaluation practice.** Accuracy comparisons need a majority-class baseline (45.5% over the 144 WVS-India items). Inflation and concentration of hard predictions should be read against baselines that show them too. Subgroup-gap claims need a permutation null and detection thresholds, which at tens of respondents exceed any plausible effect.")

H("VI. Limitations")
P("Results come from one fold, one seed and one epoch. Scoring used fp16 on a T4 while training used bf16, and the original run's greedy decoding of the same adapter scores lower (36.1%), so results depend on the readout; we use the one that matches training and the zero-shot conditions. Fine-tuning used eight religion- and fertility-related items and a full-sequence loss, so transfer to broader item sets is untested and the unseen-item null may reflect narrow training. The transfer and probability results use an MLX re-implementation of the adapter that is about 7 to 8 points weaker than the original adapter on the same rows, so the transfer comparison is conservative for the original model, and it rests on eight items and 120 respondents, with large item-level uncertainty. The local zero-shot controls use random subsets of 100 to 200 respondents, although the full-fold zero-shot result agrees. We tested one 7B model locally and two free-tier API models, and did not test frontier models on these surveys (Chen et al. [@chen2026] did, on US and cross-national data, with the same failures). We used two surveys with eight and 15 items and no language axis, and the Pew survey weights are not applied, so statements describe the sample rather than the population. Accuracy is exact match on ordinal items with argmax predictions, directional-validity cells share respondents across axes, and the variance share is an in-sample increment in R-squared.")

H("VII. Conclusions")
P("A prompted 7B model varies strongly with a demographic profile but uses it weakly and slightly harmfully. Fine-tuning on respondent-level data recovers most, though not all, of the accuracy and direction gap to classifiers on the trained questions, and shows no detectable accuracy gain on eight new ones. In the settings we tested, matched classifiers remain the benchmark that LLM respondents have to beat, and held-out questions at larger scale are the experiment most likely to change that conclusion. Code, prompts and analysis scripts are at github.com/DevilHunter695/silicon-sampling-capstone. WVS-7 must be obtained from the WVS Association and the Pew data from Pew Research Center.")
